---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro"

const REPO = "https://github.com/cdrrazan/Docaru"
---
<Base
  title="How the encryption works — Docaru"
  description="Scans are encrypted in your browser with AES-GCM under a key we never receive. What we store is ciphertext with no filename. Here is exactly how, and what it costs you."
  path="/security"
>
  <h1>How the encryption works</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Written out in full, because "encrypted" is a word every service uses and almost none of them
    mean this way. Everything below is checkable against
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/web/app/javascript/controllers/vault_controller.js`}>the source</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens when you add a scan</h2>

  <ol>
    <li>Your browser resizes and re-encodes the image. A phone photo of a passport does not need to be twelve megabytes.</li>
    <li>It encrypts the result with <strong>AES-GCM</strong>, using your vault key.</li>
    <li>It uploads the ciphertext, a nonce, and a content type. Nothing else.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The filename is not sent. Neither is the plaintext size, nor anything derived from the contents.
    There is no code path on our side that turns any of that back into a document, and adding one
    would be a change you could see in the commit history.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the key lives</h2>

  <p>
    Your scans are encrypted under a random <strong>vault key</strong>, not under your passphrase
    directly. That key is stored twice, wrapped two different ways:
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Wrapped under</th><th>Derived from</th><th>Which means</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Your passphrase</td>
          <td>PBKDF2, 310,000 iterations, SHA-256</td>
          <td>Normal unlocking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Your recovery code</td>
          <td>PBKDF2 with a separate salt</td>
          <td>Getting back in if you forget the passphrase</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Both wrappings are produced in your browser. We hold two blobs and neither key, so an operator, a
    stolen backup and a court order all get the same thing: ciphertext.
  </p>

  <div class="panel panel-warn">
    <p class="eyebrow">The part that is not reassuring</p>
    <p style="margin:0">
      Lose your passphrase <em>and</em> your recovery code and your scans are gone. Not
      "gone pending a support ticket" — gone. We cannot reset what we do not have. This is the direct
      cost of the guarantee above, and any service that offers you both a zero-knowledge promise and
      a password reset is quietly not doing one of them.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is not encrypted, and why</h2>

  <p>
    The document kind, the issuing country and the expiry date are stored in the clear, because the
    server has to compute reminder dates from them and cannot do arithmetic on ciphertext. The
    identifier — a passport number — and your notes are encrypted at rest with keys held by the
    application.
  </p>

  <p>
    One visible consequence: searching your vault covers the label, kind, country and person, and
    deliberately not the identifier or the notes. A search that reached those would require the
    server to read them. The interface says so rather than quietly returning nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What this rules out</h2>

  <p>
    No full-text search inside your documents. No previews or thumbnails. No automatic categorising,
    no OCR on our side, nothing described as smart. Every one of those features needs a copy of your
    passport that we can open. That is the trade, and it is the whole product.
  </p>

  <h2>If you find something wrong</h2>

  <p>
    Report it privately through <a href={`${REPO}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub's advisory form</a>
    rather than opening a public issue. <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>SECURITY.md</a> has
    the details and the scope.
  </p>
</Base>
